  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/... \nRidging aloft will reach peak strength over the western half of \nthe forecast area on Thursday with low-lvl thermal profiles \nresponding as 850 mb temperatures climb to around 26-27C. This \nwill result in sfc highs reaching 102-107 degrees across the \nwestern deserts, again nearing daily records in Yuma and the \nImperial Valley. Major HeatRisk is expected to be most expansive \non Thursday afternoon and therefore the Extreme Heat Watch has \nbeen expanded to include the entire Lower Colorado River valley \nand a portion of the Gila River valley in Yuma County including \nDateland and Tacna. Portions of south-central AZ will also see \nhighs climb into the low 100s or around 10 degrees above normal on\nThursday, although the HeatRisk is expected to remain in the \nModerate category. Friday will be a transition day where a \ndeepening trough over the Pac NW which in turn induces SW flow \naloft over the West Coast. Although we will begin to see a \nreduction in overall hghts aloft on Friday afternoon, low-lvl \nthermal profiles will be slow to respond as 850 mb temperatures \nare still expected to reach 25-26C across the much of the forecast\narea equating to sfc highs around 100-105 degrees in most lower \ndesert communities. Since the latest NBM forecast shows a 50-70% \nprobability of high temperatures reaching the Major HeatRisk \ncategory for the Imperial Valley and Yuma area, the decision was \nmade to extend the Extreme Heat Watch for those areas through \nFriday evening. \n\nWe then turn our attention to an upcoming pattern shift heading \ninto this weekend as most deterministic models and ensemble \nmembers are trending toward an overall troffing pattern setting up\nacross the western CONUS. There is now better model agreement \nthat a stronger and deeper trough will transition into a cutoff \nlow over the Pac NW on Saturday and gradually sink southward into \nCA Sunday and Monday. Meanwhile, latest forecast guidance from the\nNHC shows an eventual turn to the northeast by Rachel this \nweekend with the remnants of the storm itself being pulled \nnortheastward and phasing with the west coast trough. As this \npattern transpires, there should be an increase in southwesterly \nflow aloft over the Desert Southwest which will begin to pull \nPacific moisture into the region by late Saturday into Sunday. \nCoincidentally, rain chances will return to the much of the region\nwith NBM PoPs now around 20-50% across south-central AZ on Sunday\nand even up to 40-60% across the foothills NE of Phoenix by \nMonday. There is still high uncertainty in rainfall amounts at \nthis time but there is at least a chance of some higher totals \nexceeding 1.50\" across AZ high terrain. As 500 mb hghts begin to \nlower ahead of the approaching trough along with increasing cloud \ncover and moisture, high temperatures will take a dive from the \nmid 90s on Saturday to the low to mid 80s by Monday.",
